# Guard-extent / footprint / concurrency map — 2026-08-10

Instrument: `--features __probe_bounds`
(`crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/src/bounds_probe.rs`, branch `probe/bounds-map`,
base `main` @ 02dda0f, instrument commit 8c40ded).
Host: macOS aarch64 (Darwin 25.5.0), same box as the rest of the campaign.
Drivers: `examples/probe_tracker.rs` (4K AVIF cells, 3 iters after a warmup
decode) and `examples/probe_bounds_corpus.rs` (dav1d-test-data, 4 frames per
vector, all vectors of a meson group in ONE process).
Derived tables: `scripts/perf/bounds_tables.py`.
Data: `bounds_map_2026-08-10.tsv[.zst]` (derived),
`bounds_map_raw_2026-08-10.tar.zst` (the `B*` aggregate rows per cell).

## NO WALL-CLOCK NUMBER FROM THIS BUILD IS VALID

Every registration publishes a live record, executes a `SeqCst` fence and scans
every other worker's live set. `v4k_8tile` t=8 runs ~3.5x slower instrumented.
Nothing here is a timing claim.

## What was measured, and what was NOT

| | |
|---|---|
| cells | `v4k_8tile` and `v4k_8tile_10b`, t=1 and t=8; dav1d-test-data `8-bit/data` (358 vectors) at t=1 and t=8, `10-bit/data` (71) and `8-bit/features` (10) at t=8 |
| registrations | 1.57 x 10^9 across the eight cells; the largest single cell is 936,867,496 |
| loop restoration | **LIVE**: 320 of 358 `8-bit/data` vectors and 71 of 71 `10-bit/data`, 5,300,023 + 87,216 LR-site registrations. Zero on either 4K vector — those two carry no LR information at all |
| NOT measured | x86_64, wasm32, `asm`/`c-ffi`, `unchecked`, t=2/4/16, 12-bit, film-grain groups (dropped: #479 aborts them above one thread), `8-bit/{issues,size,intra,mv,mfmv,resize,vq_suite,cdfupdate,quantizer}` |

**This is an empirical map over the executions that were actually run, not a
proof.** A site that does not appear is UNKNOWN, not safe. A gap value is the
closest approach *observed*; a different vector can be closer.

## Instrument self-checks (both have teeth; both were planted and fired)

1. **Reconciliation.** `__probe_bounds` implies `probe-sites`, so both
   instruments run in one binary. Per-cell totals agree to the registration in
   all eight cells, `lost=0` on the site probe and
   `lost_slot=lost_th=lost_site=lost_inst=lost_pair=lost_tid=0` on this one:

   | cell | registrations (both instruments) |
   |---|---|
   | `v4k_8tile` t=1 | 6,005,602 |
   | `v4k_8tile` t=8 | **11,401,399** (matches the campaign's standing figure) |
   | `v4k_8tile_10b` t=1 | 6,353,270 |
   | `v4k_8tile_10b` t=8 | 12,113,063 |
   | corpus `8-bit/data` t=1 | 565,796,681 |
   | corpus `8-bit/data` t=8 | 936,867,496 |
   | corpus `10-bit/data` t=8 | 35,235,417 |
   | corpus `8-bit/features` t=8 | 11,456,905 |

   TEETH: dropping the probe's immutable acquisitions took `v4k_8tile` t=8 from
   11,401,399 to 3,604,424 against an unchanged 11,401,399 from `probe-sites`.
   Restored: identical again.

2. **`mutable_overlaps` — ground truth from the tracker.** A concurrent
   reservation overlap involving a MUTABLE record is impossible: `DisjointMut`
   panics on it. So that counter must read 0, and it does, in all eight cells,
   including over the 407,046 reservation overlaps seen in the 937 M-registration
   corpus cell (all immutable-vs-immutable: two workers reading the same
   reference-frame window — legal, and the reason a shared read region cannot be
   *owned*).

   TEETH: widening every recorded extent by +-4096 bytes took
   `mutable_overlaps` from 0 to **874** on `v4k_8tile` t=8. The conflict
   detector is not a vacuous zero — it fires the moment reservations coarsen.

   This check also FOUND a real defect in the instrument: at `NINST = 1024` the
   instance-interning table saturated on a 4K t=8 decode, distinct instances
   collapsed onto one "unknown" id, and the scan reported 70 impossible
   overlaps. Raised to 65,536 (`distinct=1030` on that cell, 7,275 on the
   corpus) with a `lost_inst` counter that must stay 0.

3. **t=1 has no concurrency**, and the map agrees: summed `n_conc` over every
   site at `v4k_8tile` t=1 is exactly 0.

## Known bias, quantified

* `lost_scan` — foreign records skipped because their seqlock generation changed
  mid-read: 684,034 of ~17.3 M foreign-slot reads on `v4k_8tile` t=8 (4.0%),
  10,610,500 of ~197 M on the corpus t=8 (5.4%). Concurrency counts are
  **under**-reported by up to that fraction.
* Footprints below `Deref` granularity are not measured. At t=8 every hot site's
  reservation is 1-16 bytes, so the unmeasured slack is bounded by ~15 bytes per
  guard — small, and it can only make over-reservation look *smaller* than it is.
* `late_stride` (an instance's row stride declared after it had been indexed) is
  3 on the single-vector cells but 1,915,051 on the corpus, because the picture
  pool and `wrap_buf` scratch recycle base addresses across vectors. The ROWBAND
  counterfactual column is therefore only trustworthy on the single-vector cells.
* The instrument perturbs scheduling, which perturbs how much genuine overlap
  occurs. Direction unknown.

## Headline results

### 1. At t=8 the shipped decoder has essentially NO over-reservation left

Every hot site reports `over_ratio = 1.000` with `fp_kind = whole` at t=8, on
both the 4K vectors and the corpus, and `never_deref = 0` everywhere: no guard
is taken purely for exclusion. Reservation means 1-16 bytes.

That is the `tile_threading_active()` gate doing its job — it already selects
the per-row (tight) path everywhere the hull path exists.

### 2. At t=1 the hull paths are live and the waste is enormous, and it is ALL inter-row gaps

`v4k_8tile` t=1 (per frame):

| site | n | reserved | footprint | over | shape of the waste |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `loopfilter.rs:769:33` (`fill_hull`) | 372,017 | 35,755.95 B | 90.71 B (10.31 rows x 10.23 B) | **394.2x** | 35,665 B of inter-row gap; lead 0, tail 0 |
| `cdef_apply.rs:121:33` | 51,824 | 26,882 | 16 (8 x 2) | **1680.1x** | 26,866 gap |
| `cdef_apply.rs:104:32` | 31,848 | 26,882 | 16 (8 x 2) | **1680.1x** | 26,866 gap |
| `safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs:622:9` | 80,864 | 26,888 | 64 (8 x 8) | 420.1x | 26,824 gap |
| `safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs:1217:9` | 30,880 | 26,888 | 64 (8 x 8) | 420.1x | 26,824 gap |
| `safe_simd/cdef_arm.rs:192:9` | 80,864 | 26,890 | 79.94 | 336.4x | 26,810 gap |

corpus `8-bit/data` t=1 (raw, 1406 frames): `cdef_apply.rs:104:32` 1014.5x,
`cdef_apply.rs:121:33` 467.2x, `cdef_arm.rs:622:9` 245.7x,
`cdef_arm.rs:1217:9` 267.1x, `loopfilter.rs:769:33` 153.1x.

**Leading and trailing waste are 0.00 at every one of these sites.** The waste
is entirely the gaps BETWEEN ROWS — #469's and #475's defect, quantified.
The tightest extent any coarsening must respect is the row set itself.

### 3. The widening budget — the table that would have predicted #485

Acquisitions whose nearest CONCURRENTLY-LIVE FOREIGN **WRITE** reservation was
within k bytes. Widening the site's reservation by k collides exactly that
often. `loopfilter.rs:710:14` is `LfBlock::fill`'s per-row read guard, the
decoder's largest single registration site.

| cell | n | <=64 B | <=256 B | <=1 KiB | <=4 KiB | <=16 KiB | <=64 KiB |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `v4k_8tile` t=8 (3 frames) | 11,505,126 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 26 | 754 |
| corpus `8-bit/data` t=8 (1406 frames) | 64,127,608 | 2 | **16** | 324 | 5,819 | 82,781 | 614,065 |
| corpus `10-bit/data` t=8 (284 frames) | 873,966 | 0 | 1 | 18 | 307 | 3,601 | 10,824 |

As a rate, at the <=4 KiB column: **0.09 collisions per million registrations on
`v4k_8tile`, 91 per million on the corpus — a factor of 1000.** The campaign's
two gap vectors under-report the collision risk by three orders of magnitude,
which is exactly why #485's column band passed its first full 4K-shaped sample
(753/755 SETDIFF CLEAN) and then failed 1-2 vectors per corpus run.

**Retrodiction of #485.** Its band copied in a 128-px row span in place of a
W in {4..16} px per-edge guard, i.e. a widening of up to ~124 bytes at 8bpc.
The <=256 B column predicts **16** collisions across 1406 frames of
`8-bit/data`. #485 measured 1, 2 and 0 errors on three full passes of the same
group. Same order, same side of zero, and available without writing the band.

The pair table names the counterparty:

| acquiring | concurrent | co-live | reservations intersect | footprints intersect | foreign is a WRITE | closest approach |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `loopfilter.rs:710:14` | `cdef_arm.rs:622:9` | 2,217,283 | 0 | 0 | yes (all) | **232 B** |
| `loopfilter.rs:710:14` | `loopfilter.rs:710:14` | 2,907,038 | 0 | 0 | no | 99 B |
| `loopfilter.rs:710:14` | `cdef_arm.rs:192:9` | 2,096,250 | 0 | 0 | no | 602 B |
| `cdef_arm.rs:1217:9` | `cdef_arm.rs:622:9` | 2,344,598 | 0 | 0 | yes (all) | 64 B |
| `mc.rs:1342:44` | `mc.rs:1342:44` | 5,508,684 | 61 | 21 | no | 0 (shared reads, legal) |

So the loop filter's read guard has ~232 bytes of headroom against the nearest
concurrent writer (CDEF's `cdef_arm.rs:622:9`) and unlimited headroom against
other readers — 128 bytes is inside the budget on average and outside it 16
times per 1406 frames, which is a decode failure 16 times.

### 4. Some sites have ZERO headroom, measured

`ctx.rs:99:27` (`CaseSet::set_disjoint`, 60.6 M registrations on the corpus):
36 acquisitions had a concurrent foreign WRITE at gap **0** — butt-adjacent —
and 263 within 4 bytes. Widening that site by ONE byte collides 36 times.

`picture.rs:2027:22` (54.1 M): 100 at gap 0, 886 within 64 bytes.

### 5. Sites that are free

`mc.rs:121:61` (181 M registrations, the largest site in the corpus) and
`mc.rs:1342:44` (67 M) have **no concurrent foreign write at any distance** —
their `no_conc_write` column equals `n`. They read reference frames, which are
immutable for the whole decode. Coarsening there cannot collide with a writer
(it can still collide with the tracker's wide path — a cost question, priced
elsewhere: #469's avalanche was 2.28x slower and #475's hull 2.65x).

## The footprint half's biggest hole, named and sized

`fp_kind = whole` means "the guard was dereferenced and declared no geometry,
so the footprint is taken to be the whole reservation". At t=8 that is nearly
exact for the hot sites because their reservations are 1-16 bytes. It is
**badly wrong at the large-reservation sites**, where the report's
`over_ratio = 1.000` is an artefact of the instrument, not a measurement:

| site | n (corpus 8-bit/data t=8) | reserved mean | footprint |
|---|---|---|---|
| `safe_simd/mc_arm.rs:5971:41` | 3,536,733 | **2,466,546 B** | NOT MEASURED (reported as 2,466,546) |
| `safe_simd/mc_arm.rs:6182:41` | 407,002 | **541,865 B** | NOT MEASURED |
| `include/dav1d/picture.rs:589:26` | 1,660,347 | 4,096 B | NOT MEASURED |
| `looprestoration.rs:408:25` / `:382:29` | 37,647 / 38,171 | ~2,050 B | NOT MEASURED |

These are whole-plane / whole-row reference reads whose kernels touch a small
window. To measure them the site has to declare its geometry the way
`narrow_guard` and `LfBlock::fill_hull` now do; until it does, treat their
`over_ratio` column as absent.

Note what it does NOT change: those sites are immutable reads of reference
frames that are immutable for the whole decode, and the concurrency half
measured them exactly — `mc.rs:121:61` and `mc.rs:1342:44` have **zero**
concurrent foreign writes at any distance. Their extent is a tracker-COST
question (#469's avalanche 2.28x, #475's hull 2.65x), not a conflict question.

## Default build is unaffected

* `--features probe-sites` census on this branch vs base 02dda0f: **identical**
  on all four 4K cells (totals, distinct count, and the sorted multiset of
  per-site count/mut/immut/mean-bytes). Line numbers shift where the no-op
  declarations were inserted, which is why the comparison is on the count
  multiset rather than on `file:line` keys.
* `cargo test --release --test decode_md5_verify`: 14 passed, 0 failed.
